{"attack": true, "case": "The target claims to dissolve the induction dilemma by treating corroboration as purely historical and making 'no claim about the future.' Yet the same mechanism states we 'choose T to act on' because it 'has proven most resilient.' Acting on a theory is inherently future-directed: deploying T for action presupposes that T's past resilience is relevant to its forthcoming performance. Without an inductive bridge from past resilience to future applicability, the 'least-worst' choice for action is unmotivated — one could equally 'act on' a theory that has never been tested, since the past record would bear no logical relation to future outcomes. Thus the target simultaneously relies on and disavows an inductive inference. The target's own countercondition concedes exactly this gap ('If resilience in the past is logically irrelevant to resilience in the future without an inductive bridge'), so the claimed dissolution is internally inconsistent: a purely historical report cannot, by itself, justify choosing T for action.", "counterexample": null, "premise": "A decision procedure that selects a theory specifically to 'act on' it necessarily projects the theory into future use, and therefore depends on a bridge from past performance to future reliability; declaring the same procedure to make 'no claim about the future' is self-contradictory.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "3dff920499b9168351449db2e49e8ae75dde569ad7653c60a8be17e91c669694", "quote": "We choose T to act on because it is the current 'least-worst' known option, the one that has proven most resilient. This avoids induction because it makes no claim about the future, only a comparative claim about the past record of resilience."}], "successor_question": "Can a purely historical 'least-worst' ranking justify acting on a theory without any tacit inductive assumption that past resilience is evidence of future reliability?"}